<%#
    THE MODEL'S PARAGRAPH, BROKEN INTO READABLE PARAGRAPHS.

    WHY THIS EXISTS. A stored reasoning is 1,200 to 2,100 characters with six to nine sentences and
    ZERO newlines - measured across every round in the demo store. Rendered in one <p> it is a wall,
    and the owner's words were "humans like some white lines in between". The 68ch measure did not
    help because nothing in the string breaks it.

    WHAT IT SPLITS ON, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. A sentence END is a period, question mark or
    exclamation mark, followed by whitespace, followed by an uppercase letter, a digit, or an
    opening quote/bracket. That is deliberately narrow:

      SPLIT      "first. Second"        "works. This is"
      NO SPLIT   "2 euros."             a period then whitespace then lowercase reads as a
                                        continuation, and a decimal or a version number has no space
      NO SPLIT   "e.g. the invoice"     lowercase after the period - so the abbreviation is absorbed
      NO SPLIT   "Mr. Smith"            capital after the period, so this ONE is split wrongly

    A SINGLE-FULL-STOP ABBREVIATION BEFORE A CAPITALISED WORD IS THE CASE IT GETS WRONG: "Mr.",
    "Dr.", "Inc.", "etc. The". It breaks a paragraph where a sentence does not end. That is accepted
    rather than repaired, and the reason is that the repair is a list of abbreviations that is wrong
    somewhere else - the codebase has been burnt by every text heuristic it has had, and a paragraph
    break in the wrong place is a cosmetic flaw, not a false claim. A wrong break cannot change what
    the sentence says.

    PARAGRAPHS ARE NEVER SHORTER THAN TWO SENTENCES, so "Mr." splitting "Mr. Smith" leaves a short
    first paragraph rather than a one-word line. Text that already carries blank lines is used as it
    is, which is what the prompt asks new rounds to produce.

    WHERE IT RENDERS. Both places the paragraph appears include this partial - the roadmap's "why it
    scored this" and the answer's "read as this process" - so they cannot drift. The Markdown export
    does not carry the reasoning at all, so there is nothing to keep in step there.
%>
<%
    function splitParagraph(text) {
        const raw = String(text === null || text === undefined ? '' : text).trim();
        if (raw === '') return [];

        // Text that already has blank lines is already paragraphed; use those breaks as they are.
        if (/\n\s*\n/.test(raw)) {
            return raw.split(/\n\s*\n/).map((part) => part.trim()).filter(Boolean);
        }

        // A sentence end: terminal punctuation, whitespace, then something that starts a sentence.
        const END = /([.!?])\s+(?=[A-Z0-9"'(\u2018\u201c])/g;
        const sentences = [];
        let last = 0;
        let match;
        while ((match = END.exec(raw)) !== null) {
            const end = match.index + 1;
            sentences.push(raw.slice(last, end).trim());
            last = match.index + match[0].length;
        }
        const tail = raw.slice(last).trim();
        if (tail) sentences.push(tail);
        if (sentences.length <= 2) return [raw];

        // Two sentences per paragraph, so a stray break from an abbreviation still leaves a
        // paragraph with content in it.
        const out = [];
        for (let i = 0; i < sentences.length; i += 2) {
            out.push(sentences.slice(i, i + 2).join(' '));
        }
        return out;
    }
%>
<% splitParagraph(reasoning).forEach(function (part) { %><p class="paragraph"><%= part %></p>
<% }); %>
